# L §8 What a result reads (tally 2.1.7 R1, issue #140; #117): `first` reads its `where` from the first key and
# `last` from the last, each stopping at the first key whose `where` is true (L §5.11); the keys after it are never
# read, so a `where` that would divide by zero there can't refuse the result. `or` stops at its first true operand.
# 2.1.6's refcore read every `where` and refused RUN-DIV.
=== m.tally
model t
  tally 2
  on 2026-03-01
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2026-12-31

source card "invented"

index ks = 1..4

input base: int

ask a: first(k in ks where k == 1 or base / (k - 2) > 0)
ask b: last(k in ks where k == 4 or base / (k - 3) > 0)
ask c: first(k in ks where k == 3 or base / (k - 2) > 0)

version v1
  cite card
  governs always
  known 2026-01-01
  base = 6 "card"
=== expect a b c
a: 1
  cut 2026-03-01
b: 4
  cut 2026-03-01
c: refused: RUN-DIV ...
  cut 2026-03-01
